<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/expert/financial-economics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Financial economics prices <strong>claims on uncertain future money</strong> — stocks, bonds,
    options — and asks what return each should earn. Its foundations: risk-averse investors,
    arbitrage (identical things must have identical prices), and the market portfolio. It is the
    most mathematical of economics' branches and the one where theory meets trillions of dollars
    daily, with measurable consequences for every pension on Earth.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the pricing framework — from diversification to factors" widget="equationStep" config={finecCfg} />

  <h2>What the theory settled and what it didn't</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Settled:</strong> diversification is (nearly) free lunch; active management mostly loses to low-cost index funds after fees (the SPIVA scorecards document this annually across markets); time diversification of equity risk.</li>
    <li><strong>Option pricing:</strong> Black–Scholes (1973, Nobel 1997) turned option valuation into a partial differential equation — and created the derivatives market it then required regulators to chase.</li>
    <li><strong>Bubbles challenge rationality:</strong> 2000 dot-com, 2008 housing. Rational-bubble models exist but strain belief; behavioural amplifiers (leverage, narratives — Shiller's "irrational exuberance", Nobel 2013) are now mainstream explanations.</li>
    <li><strong>Microstructure matters:</strong> flash crashes (2010, 2012) emerge from the plumbing — high-frequency trading, market-maker withdrawal. Market design is a live research field, not a solved one.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Markowitz (1952); Sharpe, CAPM (1964); Black & Scholes (1973); Fama & French (1993); Shiller (Nobel 2013).</p>
</TopicPage>
